\mt1 Hebrews
\c 1
\s1 The Supremacy of the Son
\r (\ref Colossians 1:15–23|COL 1:15-23\ref*)
\p \v 1 \add On many\add* past occasions and in many different ways, God spoke to \add our\add* fathers through the prophets. \v 2 But in these last days He has spoken to us by \add His\add* Son,\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Or \fqa in His Son\f* whom He appointed heir of all things, and through whom He made the universe.\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Or \fqa the world\ft ; literally \fqa the ages\f*
\p \v 3 \add The Son\add* is \add the\add* radiance of \add God’s\add* glory and \add the\add* exact representation of His nature, upholding all things by His powerful word. \add After\add* He had provided purification for sins, He sat down at \add the\add* right hand of the Majesty on high. \v 4 \add So\add* He became as far superior to the angels as \add the\add* name He has inherited \add is\add* excellent beyond theirs. \v 5 For to which of the angels did \add God\add* ever say:
\b 
\q1 “You are My Son;
\q2 today I have become Your Father”\f + \fr 1:5 \ref Psalm 2:7|PSA 2:7\ref*; literally \fqa today I have begotten You\f*?
\p \add Or\add* again:
\b 
\q1 “I will be His Father,
\q2 and He will be My Son”\f + \fr 1:5 \ref 2 Samuel 7:14|2SA 7:14\ref*; 1 Chronicles 17:13\f*?
\p \v 6 And again, when \add God\add* brings \add His\add* firstborn into the world, He says:
\b 
\q1 “\add Let\add* all God’s angels worship Him.”\f + \fr 1:6 \ref Deuteronomy 32:43|DEU 32:43\ref* (see DSS and LXX)\f*
\p \v 7 Now about the angels He says:
\b 
\q1 “He makes His angels winds,
\q2 His servants flames of fire.”\f + \fr 1:7 \ref Psalm 104:4|PSA 104:4\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 8 But about the Son \add He says\add*:
\b 
\q1 “Your throne, O God, \add endures\add* forever and ever,
\q2 and justice \add is\add* the scepter of Your kingdom.
\q1 \v 9 You have loved righteousness
\q2 and hated wickedness;
\q1 therefore God, Your God, has anointed You
\q2 above Your companions with \add the\add* oil of joy.”\f + \fr 1:9 \ref Psalm 45:6–7|PSA 45:6-7\ref*\f*
\p \v 10 And:
\b 
\q1 “In \add the\add* beginning, O Lord, You laid the foundations of the earth,
\q2 and the heavens are \add the\add* work of Your hands.
\q1 \v 11 They will perish, but You remain;
\q2 they will all wear out like a garment.
\q1 \v 12 You will roll them up like a robe;
\q2 like a garment\f + \fr 1:12 \ft BYZ and TR do not include \fqa like a garment\ft .\f* they will be changed;
\q1 but You remain the same,
\q2 and Your years will never end.”\f + \fr 1:12 \ref Psalm 102:25–27|PSA 102:25-27\ref*\f*
\p \v 13 Yet to which of the angels did \add God\add* ever say:
\b 
\q1 “Sit at My right hand
\q2 until I make Your enemies a footstool for Your feet”\f + \fr 1:13 \ref Psalm 110:1|PSA 110:1\ref*\f*?
\p \v 14 Are not \add the angels\add*\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Literally \fqa Are they not all\f* ministering spirits sent to serve those who will inherit salvation?
\c 2
\s1 Salvation Confirmed
\p \v 1 We must pay closer attention, therefore, to what we have heard, so that we do not drift away. \v 2 For if the message spoken by angels was binding, and every transgression and disobedience received \add its\add* just punishment, \v 3 how shall we escape if we neglect such a great salvation?
\p \add This salvation \add* was first announced by the Lord, was confirmed to us by those who heard \add Him\add*, \v 4 \add and\add* was affirmed by God through signs, wonders, various miracles, and \add gifts\add* of \add the\add* Holy Spirit distributed according to His will.
\s1 Jesus like His Brothers
\p \v 5 For \add it is\add* not to angels that He has subjected the world to come, about which we are speaking. \v 6 But somewhere it is testified in these words:
\b 
\q1 “What is man that You are mindful of him,
\q2 or \add the\add* son of man that You care for him?
\q1 \v 7 You made him a little lower\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Or \fqa a little while lower\ft ; also in verse 9\f* than \add the\add* angels;
\q2 You crowned him with glory and honor\f + \fr 2:7 \ft WH and TR include \fqa and set him over the works of Your hands\ft .\f*
\q2 \v 8 \add and\add* placed everything under his feet.”\f + \fr 2:8 \ref Psalm 8:4–6|PSA 8:4-6\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \add When God\add* subjected all things to him, He left nothing outside of his control. Yet at present we do not see everything subject to him. \v 9 But we see Jesus, who was made a little lower than \add the\add* angels, now crowned with glory and honor because He suffered death, so that by \add the\add* grace of God He might taste death for everyone.
\p \v 10 In bringing many sons to glory, it was fitting for \add God\add*, for whom and through whom all things \add exist\add*, to make the author\f + \fr 2:10 \ft Or \fqa pioneer \ft or \fqa founder\f* of their salvation \add perfect\add* through suffering. \v 11 For both the \add One who\add* sanctifies and those who are sanctified \add are\add* of the same \add family\add*. So \add Jesus\add* is not ashamed to call them brothers. \v 12 He says:
\b 
\q1 “I will proclaim Your name to My brothers;
\q2 I will sing Your praises in \add the\add* assembly.”\f + \fr 2:12 \ref Psalm 22:22|PSA 22:22\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 13 And again:
\b 
\q1 “I will put \add My\add* trust in Him.”\f + \fr 2:13 \ref Isaiah 8:17|ISA 8:17\ref*\f*
\p And \add once\add* again:
\b 
\q1 “Here \add am\add* I, and the children God has given Me.”\f + \fr 2:13 \ref Isaiah 8:18|ISA 8:18\ref*\f*
\p \v 14 Now since the children have flesh and blood, He too shared in \add their humanity\add*, so that by \add His\add* death He might destroy him who holds the power of death, that is, the devil, \v 15 and free those who all \add their\add* lives were held in slavery by \add their\add* fear of death.
\p \v 16 For surely \add it is\add* not \add the\add* angels He helps, but \add the\add* descendants of Abraham. \v 17 For this reason He had to be made like \add His\add* brothers in every way, so that He might become a merciful and faithful high priest \add in service\add* to God, in order to make atonement\f + \fr 2:17 \ft Or \fqa to make propitiation\f* for the sins of the people. \v 18 Because He Himself suffered when He was tempted, He is able to help those who are being tempted.
\c 3
\s1 Jesus Our Apostle and High Priest
\p \v 1 Therefore, holy brothers, who share in \add the\add* heavenly calling, set your focus on Jesus, the apostle and high priest whom we confess. \v 2 He was faithful to the \add One who\add* appointed Him, just as Moses \add was faithful\add* in all \add God’s\add* house.\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Literally \fqa just as Moses in all His house\ft ; SBL \fqa just as Moses in His house\f*
\p \v 3 For \add Jesus\add* has been counted worthy of greater glory than Moses, just as the builder \add of a house\add* has greater honor than the house \add itself\add*. \v 4 And every house is built by someone, but God \add is\add* the builder of everything.
\p \v 5 Now Moses \add was\add* faithful as a servant in all \add God’s\add* house,\f + \fr 3:5 \ref Numbers 12:7|NUM 12:7\ref*\f* testifying to what would be spoken later. \v 6 But Christ \add is faithful\add* as \add the\add* Son over \add God’s\add* house. \add And\add* we are \add His\add* house, if we hold firmly\f + \fr 3:6 \ft NE, WH, BYZ, and TR include \fqa to the end\ft .\f* to \add our\add* confidence and the hope \add of which\add* we boast.
\s1 Do Not Harden Your Hearts
\r (\ref Psalm 95:1–11|PSA 95:1-11\ref*)
\p \v 7 Therefore, as the Holy Spirit says:
\b 
\q1 “Today, if you hear His voice,
\q2 \v 8 \add do\add* not harden your hearts,
\q1 as \add you did\add* in the rebellion,
\q2 in the day of testing in the wilderness,
\q1 \v 9 where your fathers tested \add and\add* tried \add Me\add*,
\q2 and for forty years saw My works.
\q1 \v 10 Therefore I was angry with that generation,
\q2 and I said,
\q1 ‘Their hearts \add are\add* always going astray,
\q2 and they have not known My ways.’
\q1 \v 11 So I swore on oath in My anger,
\q2 ‘They shall never enter My rest.’”\f + \fr 3:11 \ref Psalm 95:7–11|PSA 95:7-11\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\s1 The Peril of Unbelief
\p \v 12 See to it, brothers, \add that none\add* of you has a wicked heart of unbelief that turns away from \add the\add* living God. \v 13 But exhort one another daily, as long as it is called today, so that none of you may be hardened by sin’s deceitfulness.
\p \v 14 We have come to share in Christ if we hold firmly to \add the\add* end the assurance \add we had\add* at first. \v 15 As it has been said:
\b 
\q1 “Today, if you hear His voice,
\q2 \add do\add* not harden your hearts,
\q1 as \add you did\add* in the rebellion.”\f + \fr 3:15 \ref Psalm 95:7–8|PSA 95:7-8\ref*\f*
\p \v 16 For who were the ones who heard \add and\add* rebelled? \add Were they\add* not all those Moses led out of Egypt? \v 17 And with whom was \add God\add* angry for forty years? \add Was it\add* not with those who sinned, whose bodies fell in the wilderness? \v 18 And to whom did He swear \add that they\add* would never enter His rest? \add Was it\add* not to those who disobeyed? \v 19 \add So\add* we see that \add it was\add* because of \add their\add* unbelief that they were unable to enter.
\c 4
\s1 The Sabbath Rest
\r (\ref Genesis 2:1–3|GEN 2:1-3\ref*; \ref Exodus 16:22–30|EXO 16:22-30\ref*)
\p \v 1 Therefore,  \add while \add* the promise of entering His rest still stands, let us be careful that none of you be deemed to have fallen short \add of it\add*. \v 2 For we also received the good news just as they \add did\add*; but the message they heard was of no value to them, since they did not share the faith of those who comprehended \add it\add*.\f + \fr 4:2 \ft Literally \fqa not having been united in the faith of those who heard\f*
\p \v 3 Now \add we\add* who have believed enter \add that\add* rest. \add As for the others, \add* it is just as \add God\add* has said:
\b 
\q1 “So I swore on oath in My anger,
\q2 ‘They shall never enter My rest.’”\f + \fr 4:3 \ref Psalm 95:11|PSA 95:11\ref*; also in verse 5\f*
\p And yet \add His\add* works have been finished since \add the\add* foundation of \add the\add* world. \v 4 For somewhere He has spoken about the seventh \add day\add* in this manner: “And on the seventh day God rested from all His works.”\f + \fr 4:4 \ref Genesis 2:2|GEN 2:2\ref*\f* \v 5 And again, \add as He says\add* in \add the passage above\add*: “They shall never enter My rest.”
\p \v 6 Since, then, it remains \add for\add* some to enter \add His rest\add*, and \add since\add* those who formerly heard the good news did not enter because of \add their\add* disobedience, \v 7 \add God\add* again designated a certain day \add as\add* “Today,” when a long time later He spoke through David as was just stated: “Today, if you hear His voice, \add do\add* not harden your hearts.”\f + \fr 4:7 \ref Psalm 95:7–8|PSA 95:7-8\ref*\f*
\p \v 8 For if Joshua had given them rest, \add God\add* would not have spoken later about another day. \v 9 There remains, then, a Sabbath rest for the people of God. \v 10 For whoever enters \add God’s\add* rest also rests from his \add own\add* work, just as God \add did\add* from \add His\add*. \v 11 Let us, therefore, make every effort to enter that rest, so that no one will fall by \add following\add* the same pattern of disobedience.
\s1 The Living Word
\r (\ref 2 Timothy 3:10–17|2TI 3:10-17\ref*)
\p \v 12 For the word of God \add is\add* living and active. Sharper than any double-edged sword, it pierces even to dividing soul and spirit, joints and marrow. It judges \add the\add* thoughts and intentions of \add the\add* heart. \v 13 Nothing in all creation is hidden from \add God’s\add* sight; everything \add is\add* uncovered and exposed before the eyes of Him to whom we \add must give\add* account.
\p \v 14 Therefore, since we have a great high priest who has passed through the heavens, Jesus the Son of God, let us hold firmly to what we profess. \v 15 For we do not have a high priest who is unable to sympathize with our weaknesses, but \add we have\add* one who was tempted in every way that \add we are\add*, \add yet was\add* without sin. \v 16 Let us then approach the throne of grace with confidence, so that we may receive mercy and find grace to help \add us\add* in \add our\add* time of need.
\c 5
\s1 The Perfect High Priest
\r (\ref Psalm 110:1–7|PSA 110:1-7\ref*)
\p \v 1 Every high priest is appointed from among men to represent \add them\add* in matters relating to God, to offer gifts and sacrifices for sins. \v 2 He is able to deal gently with those who are ignorant and misguided, since he himself is subject to weakness. \v 3 That is why he is obligated to offer sacrifices for his own sins, as well as for \add the sins of\add* the people.
\p \v 4 No one takes \add this\add* honor upon himself; he must be called by God, just as Aaron \add was\add*. \v 5 So also Christ \add did\add* not take upon Himself the glory of becoming a high priest, but \add He was called\add* by the \add One who\add* said to Him:
\b 
\q1 “You are My Son;
\q2 today I have become Your Father.”\f + \fr 5:5 \ref Psalm 2:7|PSA 2:7\ref*; literally \fqa today I have begotten You\f*
\p \v 6 And in another \add passage God\add* says:
\b 
\q1 “You \add are\add* a priest forever
\q2 in the order of Melchizedek.”\f + \fr 5:6 \ref Psalm 110:4|PSA 110:4\ref*\f*
\p \v 7 During the days of \add Jesus’\add* earthly \add life\add*, He offered up prayers and petitions with loud cries and tears to the \add One who\add* could save Him from death, and He was heard because of \add His\add* reverence. \v 8 Although He was a Son, He learned obedience from what He suffered. \v 9 And having been made perfect, He became \add the\add* source of eternal salvation to all who obey Him \v 10 \add and\add* was designated by God \add as\add* high priest in the order of Melchizedek.
\s1 Milk and Solid Food
\r (\ref 1 Corinthians 3:1–9|1CO 3:1-9\ref*)
\p \v 11 We \add have\add* much to say about \add this\add*, \add but it is\add* hard to explain, because you are dull of hearing. \v 12 Although by \add this\add* time you ought to be teachers, you need \add someone\add* to reteach you the basic principles of God’s word.\f + \fr 5:12 \ft Or \fqa of the oracles of God\f* You need milk, not solid food!
\p \v 13 For everyone who lives on milk is \add still\add* an infant, inexperienced in \add the\add* message of righteousness. \v 14 But solid food is \add for the\add* mature, who by constant use have trained \add their\add* senses to distinguish good \add from\add* evil.
\c 6
\s1 A Call to Maturity
\p \v 1 Therefore let us leave the elementary teachings about Christ \add and\add* go on to maturity, not laying again \add the\add* foundation of repentance from dead works,\f + \fr 6:1 \ft Or \fqa from acts that lead to death\f* and of faith in God, \v 2 instruction about baptisms,\f + \fr 6:2 \ft Or \fqa cleansing rites\f* \add the\add* laying on of hands, \add the\add* resurrection of \add the\add* dead, and eternal judgment. \v 3 And this we will do, if God permits.
\p \v 4 \add It is\add* impossible for those who have once been enlightened, \add who\add* have tasted the heavenly gift, \add who\add* have shared in \add the\add* Holy Spirit, \v 5 who have tasted \add the\add* goodness \add of the\add* word of God and \add the\add* powers \add of the\add* coming age — \v 6 and then have fallen away — to be restored to repentance, because they themselves are crucifying the Son of God \add all over again\add* and subjecting \add Him\add* to open shame.
\p \v 7 For land that drinks in the rain often falling on it and that produces a crop useful to those for whom it is tended receives \add the\add* blessing of God. \v 8 But \add land that\add* produces thorns and thistles \add is\add* worthless, and \add its\add* curse is imminent. \add In\add* the end it will be burned.
\p \v 9 Even though we speak like this, beloved, we are convinced of better \add things\add* in your \add case\add*— \add things that\add* accompany salvation. \v 10 For God \add is\add* not unjust. He will not forget your work and the love you have shown for His name as you have ministered to the saints and continue to \add do so\add*.
\p \v 11 We want each of you to show \add this\add* same diligence to \add the very\add* end, in order \add to make your\add* hope sure. \v 12 Then you will not be sluggish, but will imitate those who through faith and patience inherit what has been promised.
\s1 God’s Unchangeable Promise
\p \v 13 \add When\add* God made His promise to Abraham, since He had no one greater to swear by, He swore by Himself, \v 14 saying, “\add I will\add* surely bless you and multiply your \add descendants\add*.”\f + \fr 6:14 \ref Genesis 22:17|GEN 22:17\ref*\f* \v 15 And so \add Abraham\add*, after waiting patiently, obtained the promise.
\p \v 16 Men swear by \add someone\add* greater \add than themselves\add*, and \add their\add* oath serves as a confirmation to end all argument. \v 17 So when God wanted to make the unchanging nature of His purpose very clear to the heirs of the promise, He guaranteed \add it\add* with an oath. \v 18 Thus by two unchangeable things in which \add it is\add* impossible \add for\add* God to lie, we who have fled to take hold of the hope set before \add us\add* may be strongly encouraged.
\p \v 19 We have \add this hope\add* as an anchor for the soul, firm and secure. It enters the inner \add sanctuary behind\add* the curtain, \v 20 where Jesus \add our\add* forerunner has entered on our behalf. He has become a high priest forever in the order of Melchizedek.
\c 7
\s1 Melchizedek and Abraham
\r (\ref Genesis 14:17–24|GEN 14:17-24\ref*)
\p \v 1 This Melchizedek \add was\add* king of Salem \add and\add* priest of God Most High.\f + \fr 7:1 \ref Genesis 14:18|GEN 14:18\ref*\f* He met Abraham returning from the slaughter of the kings and blessed him, \v 2 and Abraham apportioned to \add him\add* a tenth of everything. First, \add his name\add* means “king of righteousness.” Then also, “king of Salem” means “king of peace.” \v 3 Without father \add or\add* mother \add or\add* genealogy, without beginning of days \add or\add* end of life, like the Son of God, he remains a priest for all time.
\p \v 4 Consider how great \add Melchizedek was\add*: Even the patriarch Abraham gave \add him\add* a tenth of the plunder. \v 5 Now the law commands the sons of Levi who become priests to collect a tenth from the people — that is, from their brothers — though they \add too\add* are descended from Abraham. \v 6 But \add Melchizedek\add*, who did not trace his descent from \add Levi\add*, collected a tenth from Abraham and blessed him who had the promises. \v 7 And indisputably, the lesser is blessed by the greater.
\p \v 8 In the case \add of the Levites\add*, mortal men collect the tenth; but in the case \add of Melchizedek\add*, it is affirmed that he lives on. \v 9 And so to speak, Levi, who collects \add the\add* tenth, paid \add the\add* tenth through Abraham. \v 10 For when Melchizedek met Abraham, \add Levi\add* was still in the loin of \add his\add* ancestor.
\s1 A Superior Priesthood
\p \v 11 Now if perfection could have been attained through the Levitical priesthood (for on this basis the people received \add the\add* law), why \add was there\add* still need \add for\add* another priest to appear — \add one\add* in the order of Melchizedek and not in the order of Aaron? \v 12 For \add when\add* the priesthood is changed, \add the\add* law must be changed as well.
\p \v 13 \add He\add* of whom these things are said belonged to a different tribe, from which no one has ever served at the altar. \v 14 For \add it is\add* clear that our Lord descended from Judah, a tribe as to which Moses said nothing about priests.
\p \v 15 And  \add this point \add* is even more clear if another priest like Melchizedek appears, \v 16 one who has become \add a priest\add* not by a law of succession, but by \add the\add* power of an indestructible life. \v 17 For it is testified:
\b 
\q1 “You \add are\add* a priest forever
\q2 in the order of Melchizedek.”\f + \fr 7:17 \ref Psalm 110:4|PSA 110:4\ref*\f*
\p \v 18 So the former commandment is set aside because it \add was\add* weak and useless \v 19 (for the law made nothing perfect), and a better hope \add is\add* introduced, by which we draw near to God.
\p \v 20 And none of this \add happened\add* without an oath. For \add others\add* became priests without an oath, \v 21 but \add Jesus became a priest\add* with an oath by the \add One who\add* said to Him:
\b 
\q1 “\add The\add* Lord has sworn and will not change His mind:
\q2 ‘You \add are\add* a priest forever.’”\f + \fr 7:21 \ref Psalm 110:4|PSA 110:4\ref*\f*
\p \v 22 Because of this \add oath\add*, Jesus has become \add the\add* guarantee of a better covenant.
\p \v 23 Now there have been many \add other\add* priests, since death prevented them from continuing \add in office\add*. \v 24 But because \add Jesus\add* lives forever, He has a permanent priesthood. \v 25 Therefore He is able to save completely\f + \fr 7:25 \ft Or \fqa forever\f* those who draw near to God through Him, \add since He\add* always lives to intercede for them.
\p \v 26 Such a high priest truly befits us — \add One who is\add* holy, innocent, undefiled, set apart from sinners, and exalted above the heavens. \v 27 \add Unlike\add* the \add other\add* high priests, \add He\add* does not need to offer daily sacrifices, first for \add His\add* own sins and then for \add the sins\add* of the people; He \add sacrificed for sin\add* once for all when He offered up Himself. \v 28 For the law appoints as high priests men who are weak; but the oath, which \add came\add* after the law, \add appointed the\add* Son, who has been made perfect forever.
\c 8
\s1 Christ’s Eternal Priesthood
\p \v 1 \add The\add* point of what we are saying \add is this\add*: We do have such a high priest, who sat down at \add the\add* right hand of the throne of the Majesty in heaven, \v 2 \add and who\add* ministers in the sanctuary and true tabernacle set up by the Lord, not by man. \v 3 And since every high priest is appointed to offer both gifts and sacrifices, \add it was\add* necessary \add for\add* this One also to have something to offer.
\p \v 4 Now if He were on earth, He would not be a priest, since there are already \add priests\add* who offer gifts according to \add the\add* law. \v 5 \add The place where\add* they serve \add is\add* a copy and shadow of what is in heaven. This is why Moses was warned when he was about to build the tabernacle: “See to it that you make everything according to the pattern shown you on the mountain.”\f + \fr 8:5 \ref Exodus 25:40|EXO 25:40\ref*; see also Exodus 26:30.\f*
\s1 The New Covenant
\r (\ref Jeremiah 31:26–40|JER 31:26-40\ref*)
\p \v 6 Now, however, \add Jesus\add* has received a much more excellent ministry, just as the covenant He mediates is better \add and\add* is founded on better promises. \v 7 For if that first  \add covenant \add* had been without fault, no place would have been sought for a second. \v 8 \add But God\add* found fault with \add the people and\add* said:
\b 
\q1 “Behold, \add the\add* days are coming, declares \add the\add* Lord,
\q2 \add when\add* I will make a new covenant
\q1 with the house of Israel
\q2 and with the house of Judah.
\q1 \v 9 \add It will\add* not be like the covenant
\q2 I made with their fathers
\q1 when I took them by the hand
\q2 to lead them out of \add the\add* land of Egypt,
\q1 because they did not abide by My covenant,
\q2 and I disregarded them,
\qr declares \add the\add* Lord.
\q1 \v 10 For this \add is\add* the covenant I will make
\q2 with the house of Israel
\q1 after those days,
\q2 declares \add the\add* Lord.
\q1 I will put My laws in their minds
\q2 and inscribe them on their hearts.
\q1 And I will be their God,
\q2 and they will be My people.
\q1 \v 11 No longer \add will\add* each one teach his neighbor \add or\add* his brother,
\q2 saying, ‘Know the Lord,’
\q1 because they will all know Me,
\q2 from \add the\add* least of them to \add the\add* greatest.
\q1 \v 12 For I will forgive their iniquities
\q2 and will remember their sins no more.”\f + \fr 8:12 \ref Jeremiah 31:31–34|JER 31:31-34\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 13 By speaking of a new \add covenant\add*,\f + \fr 8:13 \ft Literally \fqa In saying new\ft ; here and in \ref Hebrews 9:1|HEB 9:1\ref* and 18, \fqa covenant \ft is included for clarity but is not contained in the Greek. A broader interpretation could also include \fqa priesthood \ft or \fqa tabernacle\ft .\f* He has made the first one \add obsolete\add*; and what is obsolete and aging will soon disappear.
\c 9
\s1 The Earthly Tabernacle
\r (\ref Exodus 40:1–33|EXO 40:1-33\ref*; \ref Acts 7:44–47|ACT 7:44-47\ref*)
\p \v 1 Now the first  \add covenant \add*\f + \fr 9:1 \ft Literally \fqa the first\ft ; also in verse 18; see the footnote for \ref Hebrews 8:13|HEB 8:13\ref*.\f* had regulations for worship and also an earthly sanctuary. \v 2 A tabernacle was prepared. In \add its\add* first \add room were\add* the lampstand, the table, and the consecrated bread.\f + \fr 9:2 \ft Or \fqa the Bread of the Presence\f* This was called \add the\add* Holy \add Place\add*. \v 3 Behind the second curtain \add was\add* a room called \add the\add* Most Holy Place,\f + \fr 9:3 \ft Or \fqa the Holy of Holies\f* \v 4 containing \add the\add* golden altar of incense and the gold-covered ark of the covenant. Inside \add the ark were the\add* gold jar of manna, Aaron’s staff that had budded, and the stone tablets of the covenant. \v 5 Above \add the ark were the\add* cherubim of glory, overshadowing the mercy seat.\f + \fr 9:5 \ft Or \fqa atonement cover\f* \add But\add* we cannot discuss \add these things\add* in detail now.
\p \v 6 \add When\add* everything had been prepared in this way, the priests entered regularly into the first room to perform \add their\add* sacred duties. \v 7 But only the high priest \add entered\add* the second \add room\add*, \add and then\add* only once a year, \add and\add* never without blood, which he offered for himself and for the sins the people had committed in ignorance.
\p \v 8 By this \add arrangement\add* the Holy Spirit was showing \add that\add* the way into the \add Most\add* Holy Place\f + \fr 9:8 \ft Or \fqa the Holy Place\ft ; also in verses 12 and 25\f* \add had\add* not yet been disclosed \add as long as\add* the first tabernacle was still standing. \v 9 \add It is\add* an illustration for the present time, because \add the\add* gifts and sacrifices being offered were unable to cleanse \add the\add* conscience of the worshiper. \v 10  \add They consist \add* only in food and drink and special washings — external regulations imposed until \add the\add* time of reform.
\s1 Redemption through His Blood
\p \v 11 But \add when\add* Christ came as high priest of the good things that have come,\f + \fr 9:11 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa that are to come\f* \add He went through\add* the greater and more perfect tabernacle that is not made by hands \add and is\add* not \add a part\add* of this creation. \v 12 \add He did not enter\add* by \add the\add* blood of goats and calves, but He entered the \add Most\add* Holy Place once for all by \add His\add* own blood, thus securing eternal redemption.
\p \v 13 For if the blood of goats and bulls and \add the\add* ashes of a heifer sprinkled on those who are ceremonially unclean sanctify \add them\add* so that \add their\add* bodies are clean, \v 14 how much more \add will\add* the blood of Christ, who through \add the\add* eternal Spirit offered Himself unblemished to God, purify our\f + \fr 9:14 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa your\f* consciences from works of death, so that we may serve \add the\add* living God!
\p \v 15 Therefore \add Christ\add* is \add the\add* mediator of a new covenant, so that those who are called may receive the promised eternal inheritance, now that He has died to redeem \add them\add* from the transgressions \add committed\add* under the first covenant.
\p \v 16 In the case of \add a\add* will,\f + \fr 9:16 \ft Greek \fqa diathēkē \ft is also translated as \fqa covenant \ft throughout this chapter.\f* \add it is\add* necessary to establish \add the\add* death of the \add one who\add* made \add it\add*, \v 17 because a will \add does not\add* take effect until the \add one who\add* made \add it\add* has died; it cannot be executed while he is still alive.
\p \v 18 That is why \add even\add* the first \add covenant\add* was not put into effect without blood. \v 19 For \add when\add* Moses had proclaimed every commandment of the law to all the people, he took the blood of calves and goats,\f + \fr 9:19 \ft SBL does not include \fqa and goats\ft .\f* along with water, scarlet wool, and hyssop, \add and\add* sprinkled the scroll and all the people, \v 20 saying, “This \add is\add* the blood of the covenant, which God has commanded you \add to keep\add*.”\f + \fr 9:20 \ref Exodus 24:8|EXO 24:8\ref*\f*
\p \v 21 In the same way, he sprinkled with blood the tabernacle and all the vessels used in worship. \v 22 According to the law, in fact, nearly everything must be purified with blood, and without the shedding of blood there is no forgiveness.
\p \v 23 So \add it was\add* necessary \add for\add* the copies \add of\add* the heavenly things to be purified with these \add sacrifices\add*, but the heavenly things themselves with better sacrifices than these. \v 24 For Christ did not enter a man-made copy of the true sanctuary, but \add He entered\add* heaven itself, now to appear on our behalf in the presence of God.
\p \v 25 Nor \add did He enter heaven\add* to offer Himself again and again, as the high priest enters the \add Most\add* Holy Place every year with blood that is not his own. \v 26 Otherwise, \add Christ\add* would have had to suffer repeatedly since \add the\add* foundation of \add the\add* world. But now He has appeared once \add for all\add* at \add the\add* end of the ages to do away with sin by the sacrifice of Himself.
\p \v 27 Just as man is appointed to die once, and after that \add to face\add* judgment, \v 28 so also Christ was offered once to bear \add the\add* sins of many; \add and\add* He will appear a second time, not to bear sin, \add but\add* to \add bring\add* salvation to those who eagerly await Him.
\c 10
\s1 Christ’s Perfect Sacrifice
\r (\ref Psalm 40:1–17|PSA 40:1-17\ref*)
\p \v 1 For the law is only a shadow of the good things to come, not the realities themselves. It can never, by the same sacrifices offered year after year, make perfect those who draw near  \add to worship \add*. \v 2 \add If it could\add*, would not the offerings have ceased? For the worshipers would have been cleansed once \add for all\add*, \add and\add* would no longer have felt the guilt of \add their\add* sins.
\p \v 3 Instead, those \add sacrifices are\add* an annual reminder of sins, \v 4 because \add it is\add* impossible \add for the\add* blood of bulls and goats to take away sins. \v 5 Therefore, when \add Christ\add* came into the world, He said:
\q1 \wj “Sacrifice and offering You did not desire,\wj*
\q2 \wj but a body You prepared for Me.\wj*
\q1 \v 6 \wj In burnt offerings and sin \add offerings\add*\wj*
\q2 \wj You took no delight.\wj*
\q1 \v 7 \wj Then I said, ‘Here I am, it is written about Me in \add the\add* scroll:\wj*
\q2 \wj I have come to do Your will, O God.’”\f + \fr 10:7 \ref Psalm 40:6–8|PSA 40:6-8\ref* (see also LXX)\f*\wj*
\p \v 8  \add In the passage \add* above He says, \wj “Sacrifices and offerings, burnt offerings and sin \add offerings\add* You did not desire, nor did You delight in \add them\add*”\wj* (\add although\add* they are offered according to \add the\add* law). \v 9 Then He adds, \wj “Here I am, I have come to do Your will.”\wj* He takes away the first to establish the second. \v 10 \add And\add* by that will, we have been sanctified through the sacrifice of the body of Jesus Christ once for all.
\p \v 11 Day after day every priest stands to minister and to offer again and again the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins. \v 12 But \add when\add* this \add Priest\add* had offered for all time one sacrifice for sins, He sat down at \add the\add* right hand of God. \v 13 Since that time, He waits for His enemies to be made a footstool for His feet, \v 14 because by a single offering He has made perfect for all time those who are being sanctified.
\p \v 15 The Holy Spirit also testifies to us \add about this\add*. \add First\add* He says:
\b 
\q1 \v 16 “This \add is\add* the covenant I will make with them
\q2 after those days, declares \add the\add* Lord.
\q1 I will put My laws in their hearts
\q2 and inscribe them on their minds.”\f + \fr 10:16 \ref Jeremiah 31:33|JER 31:33\ref*\f*
\p \v 17 \add Then He adds\add*:
\b 
\q1 “Their sins and lawless acts
\q2 I will remember no more.”\f + \fr 10:17 \ref Jeremiah 31:34|JER 31:34\ref*\f*
\p \v 18 And where these have been forgiven, an offering for sin \add is\add* no longer \add needed \add*.
\s1 A Call to Persevere
\r (\ref Jude 1:17–23|JUD 17-23\ref*)
\p \v 19 Therefore, brothers, \add since\add* we have confidence to enter the \add Most\add* Holy Place\f + \fr 10:19 \ft Or \fqa the Holy Place\f* by the blood of Jesus, \v 20 by \add the\add* new and living way opened for us through the curtain of His body,\f + \fr 10:20 \ft Literally \fqa through the veil that is His flesh\f* \v 21 and \add since we have\add* a great priest over the house of God, \v 22 let us draw near with a sincere heart in full assurance of faith, \add having\add* our hearts sprinkled to cleanse us from a guilty conscience and \add our\add* bodies washed with pure water.
\p \v 23 Let us hold resolutely to the hope we profess, for He who promised \add is\add* faithful. \v 24 And let us consider how to spur one another \add on\add* to love and good deeds. \v 25 Let us not neglect meeting together, as some \add have made a\add* habit, but let us encourage \add one another\add*, and all \add the\add* more as you see the Day approaching.
\p \v 26 \add If\add* we deliberately go on sinning after we have received the knowledge of the truth, no further sacrifice for sins remains, \v 27 but only a fearful expectation of judgment and of raging fire that will consume \add all\add* adversaries. \v 28 Anyone who rejected \add the\add* law of Moses died without mercy on \add the testimony of\add* two or three witnesses. \v 29 How much more severely do you think one deserves to be punished \add who\add* has trampled on the Son of God, profaned the blood of the covenant that sanctified him, and insulted the Spirit of grace?
\p \v 30 For we know Him who said, “Vengeance \add is\add* Mine; I will repay,”\f + \fr 10:30 \ref Deuteronomy 32:35|DEU 32:35\ref* (see also LXX)\f* and again, “\add The\add* Lord will judge His people.”\f + \fr 10:30 \ref Deuteronomy 32:36|DEU 32:36\ref*; Psalm 135:14\f* \v 31 \add It is\add* a fearful thing to fall into \add the\add* hands of \add the\add* living God.
\p \v 32 Remember the early days that you were in the light.\f + \fr 10:32 \ft Or \fqa Remember when you were first enlightened.\f* In \add those days\add*, you endured a great conflict in the face of suffering. \v 33 Sometimes you were publicly exposed to ridicule and persecution; at other times you were partners with those who \add were\add* so treated. \v 34 You sympathized with those in prison and joyfully accepted the confiscation of your property, knowing that you yourselves had a better and permanent possession.
\p \v 35 So \add do\add* not throw away your confidence; \add it\add* holds a great reward. \v 36 You need to persevere, so that after you have done the will of God, you will receive what He has promised. \v 37 For,
\b 
\q1 “In just a little while,
\q2 He who is coming will come and will not delay.
\q1 \v 38 But My righteous one will live by faith;\f + \fr 10:38 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa But the righteous will live by faith\f*
\q2 and if he shrinks back,
\q2 I \add will take\add* no pleasure in him.”\f + \fr 10:38 \ref Habakkuk 2:3–4|HAB 2:3-4\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 39 But we are not of \add those who\add* shrink back \add and\add* are destroyed, but of \add those who have\add* faith \add and\add* preserve \add their\add* souls.
\c 11
\s1 Faith and Assurance
\r (\ref Genesis 1:1–2|GEN 1:1-2\ref*; \ref John 1:1–5|JHN 1:1-5\ref*)
\p \v 1 Now faith is \add the\add* assurance of what \add we\add* hope for \add and the\add* certainty of what we do not see. \v 2 This is why the ancients were commended.
\p \v 3 By faith we understand that the universe was formed at God’s command, so that what is seen was not made out of what was visible.
\s1 The Faith of Abel, Enoch, Noah
\r (\ref Genesis 4–9\ref*)
\p \v 4 By faith Abel offered God a better sacrifice than Cain \add did\add*. By \add faith\add* he was commended as righteous \add when\add* God gave approval to his gifts. And by \add faith\add* he still speaks, \add even though\add* he is dead.
\p \v 5 By faith Enoch was taken up\f + \fr 11:5 \ft Literally \fqa transferred \ft or \fqa translated\f* \add so that\add* he did not see death: “He could not be found, because God had taken him away.”\f + \fr 11:5 \ref Genesis 5:24|GEN 5:24\ref* (see also LXX)\f* For before he was taken, he was commended as one who pleased God.
\p \v 6 And without faith \add it is\add* impossible to please \add God\add*. For anyone who approaches \add Him\add* must believe that He exists and \add that\add* He rewards those who earnestly seek Him.
\p \v 7 By faith Noah, when warned about things not yet seen, in godly fear built an ark to save his family. By \add faith\add* he condemned the world and became heir of the righteousness \add that\add* comes by faith.
\s1 The Faith of Abraham and Sarah
\r (\ref Genesis 15–22\ref*; \ref Romans 4:1–12|ROM 4:1-12\ref*)
\p \v 8 By faith Abraham, when called to go to a place he would later receive as \add his\add* inheritance, obeyed and went, without knowing where he was going. \v 9 By faith he dwelt in the promised land as \add a stranger\add* in a foreign \add country\add*. He lived in tents, as did Isaac and Jacob, who \add were\add* heirs with him of the same promise. \v 10 For he was looking forward to the city with foundations, whose architect and builder \add is\add* God.
\p \v 11 By faith Sarah, even though she was barren and beyond the proper age, was enabled to conceive a child, because she considered Him faithful who had promised. \v 12 And so from one man, and he as good as dead, came descendants as numerous as the stars in the sky and as countless as the sand on the seashore.
\p \v 13 All these \add people\add* died in faith, without having received the things they were promised. However, they saw \add them\add* and welcomed them from afar. And they acknowledged that they were strangers and exiles on the earth.
\p \v 14 Now those who say such things show that they are seeking a country of their own. \v 15 If they had been thinking of \add the country\add* they had left, they would have had opportunity to return. \v 16 Instead, they were longing for a better \add country\add*, a heavenly \add one\add*. Therefore God is not ashamed to be called their God, for He has prepared a city for them.
\p \v 17 By faith Abraham, when he was tested, offered up Isaac  \add on the altar \add*. He who had received the promises was ready to offer \add his\add* one and only son,\f + \fr 11:17 \ft Or \fqa only begotten son \ft or \fqa unique son\f* \v 18 even though \add God\add* had said to him, “Through Isaac your offspring will be reckoned.”\f + \fr 11:18 \ref Genesis 21:12|GEN 21:12\ref*\f* \v 19 \add Abraham\add* reasoned that God could raise \add the\add* dead, \add and\add* in a sense, he did receive \add Isaac\add* back from \add death\add*.
\s1 The Faith of Isaac, Jacob, and Joseph
\r (\ref Genesis 27–50\ref*)
\p \v 20 By faith Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau concerning the future.
\p \v 21 By faith Jacob, when he was dying, blessed each of Joseph’s sons and worshiped \add as he leaned\add* on the top of his staff.
\p \v 22 By faith Joseph, when his end was near, spoke about the exodus of the Israelites and gave instructions about his bones.
\s1 The Faith of Moses
\r (\ref Exodus 2–15\ref*; \ref Acts 7:20–22|ACT 7:20-22\ref*)
\p \v 23 By faith \add Moses’\add* parents hid him for three months after his birth, because they saw \add that he was\add* a beautiful child, and they were unafraid of the king’s edict.
\p \v 24 By faith Moses, when he was grown, refused to be called \add the\add* son of Pharaoh’s daughter. \v 25 He chose to suffer oppression with God’s people rather than to experience \add the\add* fleeting enjoyment of sin. \v 26 He valued disgrace for Christ \add above\add* the treasures of Egypt, for he was looking ahead to \add his\add* reward.
\p \v 27 By faith \add Moses\add* left Egypt, not fearing the king’s anger; he persevered because he saw \add Him who is\add* invisible. \v 28 By faith he kept the Passover and the sprinkling of blood, so that the destroyer \add of\add* the firstborn would not touch \add Israel’s own firstborn \add*.
\p \v 29 By faith \add the people\add* passed through the Red Sea as on dry land; \add but when\add* the Egyptians tried to follow,\f + \fr 11:29 \ft Literally \fqa made an attempt\f* they were drowned.
\s1 The Faith of Many
\p \v 30 By faith the walls of Jericho fell, after \add the people\add* had marched around them for seven days.
\p \v 31 By faith the prostitute Rahab, because she welcomed the spies in peace, did not perish with those who were disobedient.
\p \v 32 And what more shall I say? Time will not allow me to tell of Gideon, Barak, Samson, Jephthah, David, Samuel, and the prophets, \v 33 who through faith conquered kingdoms, administered justice, \add and\add* gained what was promised; \add who\add* shut \add the\add* mouths of lions, \v 34 quenched \add the\add* raging fire, \add and\add* escaped \add the\add* edge of \add the\add* sword; \add who\add* gained strength from weakness, became mighty in battle, \add and\add* put foreign armies \add to flight\add*.
\p \v 35 Women received back their dead, raised to life again. Others were tortured \add and\add* refused \add their\add* release, so that they might gain a better resurrection. \v 36 \add Still\add* others endured mocking and flogging, and even chains and imprisonment.
\p \v 37 They were stoned, they were sawed in two,\f + \fr 11:37 \ft NE, WH, BYZ, and TR include \fqa they were put to the test,\f* they were put to death by \add the\add* sword. They went around in sheepskins \add and\add* goatskins, destitute, oppressed, \add and\add* mistreated. \v 38 The world was not worthy of \add them\add*. They wandered in deserts and mountains, and \add hid in \add* caves and holes in the ground.
\p \v 39 These \add were\add* all commended for \add their\add* faith, \add yet\add* they did not receive what \add was\add* promised. \v 40 God had planned something better for us, so that together with us they would be made perfect.
\c 12
\s1 A Call to Endurance
\r (\ref 2 Timothy 2:1–13|2TI 2:1-13\ref*)
\p \v 1 Therefore, \add since\add* we are surrounded by such a great cloud of witnesses, let us throw off every encumbrance and the sin \add that\add* so easily entangles, \add and\add* let us run with endurance the race set out for us. \v 2 Let us fix our eyes on Jesus, the author\f + \fr 12:2 \ft Or \fqa pioneer \ft or \fqa founder\f* and perfecter of \add our\add* faith, who for the joy set before Him endured \add the\add* cross, scorning \add its\add* shame, and sat down at \add the\add* right hand of the throne of God. \v 3 Consider Him who endured such hostility from sinners, so that you will not grow weary \add and\add* lose heart.
\s1 God Disciplines His Sons
\p \v 4 In your struggle against sin, you have not yet resisted to the point of shedding your blood. \v 5 And you have forgotten the exhortation that addresses you as sons:
\b 
\q1 “My son, \add do\add* not take lightly \add the\add* discipline of \add the\add* Lord,
\q2 and \add do not\add* lose heart \add when\add* He rebukes you.
\q1 \v 6 For \add the\add* Lord disciplines the one He loves,
\q2 and He chastises every son He receives.”\f + \fr 12:6 \ft Literally \fqa and He flogs every son He receives\ft ; \ref Proverbs 3:11–12|PRO 3:11-12\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 7 Endure suffering as discipline; God is treating you as sons. For what son is not disciplined \add by his\add* father? \v 8 If you do not experience discipline like everyone else, then you are illegitimate children and not true sons. \v 9 Furthermore, we have all had earthly fathers who disciplined \add us\add*, and we respected \add them\add*. \add Should we\add* not much more submit to the Father of \add our\add* spirits and live?
\p \v 10 \add Our fathers\add* disciplined \add us\add* for a short time as they thought best, but \add God disciplines us\add* for \add our\add* good, so that we may share in His holiness. \v 11 No discipline seems enjoyable at the time, but painful. Later on, however, it yields a harvest of righteousness \add and\add* peace\f + \fr 12:11 \ft Or \fqa it yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness\f* to those who have been trained by it.
\p \v 12 Therefore strengthen \add your\add* limp hands and weak knees.\f + \fr 12:12 \ref Isaiah 35:3|ISA 35:3\ref*\f* \v 13 Make straight paths for your feet,\f + \fr 12:13 \ref Proverbs 4:26|PRO 4:26\ref* (see also LXX)\f* so that the lame \add may\add* not be disabled, but rather healed.
\s1 A Call to Holiness
\r (\ref 1 Peter 1:13–21|1PE 1:13-21\ref*)
\p \v 14 Pursue peace with everyone, as well as holiness, without which no one will see the Lord. \v 15 See to it that no one falls short of the grace of God, \add and\add* that no root of bitterness\f + \fr 12:15 \ft See \ref Deuteronomy 29:18|DEU 29:18\ref*\f* springs up to cause trouble and defile many. \v 16 \add See to it\add* that \add no one is\add* sexually immoral, or is godless like Esau, who for a single meal sold his birthright. \v 17 For you know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing, he was rejected. He could find no ground for repentance, though he sought \add the blessing\add* with tears.
\s1 An Unshakable Kingdom
\r (\ref Exodus 20:18–21|EXO 20:18-21\ref*; \ref Deuteronomy 5:22–33|DEU 5:22-33\ref*)
\p \v 18 For you have not come to  \add a mountain that \add* can be touched and \add that\add*\f + \fr 12:18 \ft Literally \fqa to what can be touched and\f* is burning with fire; to darkness, gloom, and storm; \v 19 to a trumpet blast \add or\add* to a voice that \add made its\add* hearers beg that no further word be spoken. \v 20 For they could not bear what was commanded: “If even an animal touches the mountain, it must be stoned.”\f + \fr 12:20 \ref Exodus 19:12–13|EXO 19:12-13\ref*\f* \v 21 The sight was so terrifying \add that\add* even Moses said, “I am trembling with fear.”\f + \fr 12:21 \ref Deuteronomy 9:19|DEU 9:19\ref*\f*
\p \v 22 Instead, you have come to Mount Zion, \add to the\add* city of \add the\add* living God, \add the\add* heavenly Jerusalem. \add You have come\add* to myriads of angels \v 23 in joyful assembly, \add to the\add* congregation of \add the\add* firstborn, enrolled in heaven. \add You have come\add* to God \add the\add* Judge of all, to \add the\add* spirits of \add the\add* righteous made perfect, \v 24 to Jesus \add the\add* mediator of a new covenant, and to \add the\add* sprinkled blood that speaks a better word than \add the blood\add* of Abel.
\p \v 25 See to it that you do not refuse Him who speaks. For if \add the people did\add* not escape when they refused Him who warned \add them\add* on earth, how much less \add will\add* we \add escape\add* if we reject Him who \add warns us\add* from heaven? \v 26 At that time \add His\add* voice shook the earth, but now He has promised, “Once more I will shake not only the earth, but heaven as well.”\f + \fr 12:26 \ref Haggai 2:6|HAG 2:6\ref*\f* \v 27 The \add words\add* “Once more” signify the removal of what can be shaken — that is, created things — so that the unshakable may remain.
\p \v 28 Therefore, \add since\add* we are receiving an unshakable kingdom, let us be filled with gratitude, \add and so\add* worship God acceptably with reverence and awe. \v 29 “For our God \add is a\add* consuming fire.”\f + \fr 12:29 \ref Deuteronomy 4:24|DEU 4:24\ref*\f*
\c 13
\s1 Brotherly Love
\p \v 1 Continue in brotherly love. \v 2 \add Do\add* not neglect to show hospitality to strangers, for by so doing some \add people\add* have entertained angels without knowing it. \v 3 Remember those in prison as if you were bound with \add them\add*, \add and\add* those who are mistreated as if you were \add suffering with them\add*.
\p \v 4 Marriage should be honored by all and the marriage bed kept undefiled, for God will judge the sexually immoral and adulterers.
\s1 Christ’s Unchanging Nature
\p \v 5 Keep your lives free from the love of money \add and\add* be content with what you have, for \add God\add* has said:
\b 
\q1 “Never will I leave you,
\q2 never will I forsake you.”\f + \fr 13:5 \ref Deuteronomy 31:6|DEU 31:6\ref*, 8; Joshua 1:5\f*
\p \v 6 So we say with confidence:
\b 
\q1 “\add The\add* Lord \add is\add* my helper; I will not be afraid.
\q2 What \add can\add* man do to me?”\f + \fr 13:6 \ref Psalm 118:6|PSA 118:6\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 7 Remember your leaders who spoke the word of God to you. Consider the outcome of \add their\add* way of life \add and\add* imitate \add their\add* faith. \v 8 Jesus Christ \add is\add* the same yesterday and today and forever.
\p \v 9 \add Do\add* not be carried away \add by\add* all kinds \add of\add* strange teachings, for \add it is\add* good for the heart to be strengthened by grace \add and\add* not by foods of no value to those devoted to \add them\add*. \v 10 We have an altar from which those who serve at the tabernacle have no right to eat.
\p \v 11 \add Although\add* the high priest brings the blood of animals into the Holy Place \add as a sacrifice\add* for sin, the bodies are burned outside the camp. \v 12 And so Jesus \add also\add* suffered outside the \add city\add* gate, to sanctify the people by \add His\add* own blood. \v 13 Therefore let us go to Him outside the camp, bearing the disgrace He \add bore\add*. \v 14 For here we do not have a permanent city, but we are looking for the \add city\add* that is to come.
\s1 Sacrifice, Obedience, and Prayer
\p \v 15 Through \add Jesus\add*, therefore, let us continually offer to God \add a\add* sacrifice of praise, \add the\add* fruit of lips that confess His name. \v 16 And \add do\add* not neglect to do good and to share with others, for with such sacrifices God is pleased.
\p \v 17 Obey your leaders and submit \add to them\add*, for they watch over your souls as those who must give an account. To this end, allow them \add to lead\add* with joy and not \add with\add* grief, for that \add would be\add* of no advantage to you.
\p \v 18 Pray for us; we are convinced that we have a clear conscience \add and\add* desire to live honorably in every way. \v 19 And I especially urge you to \add pray\add* that I may be restored to you soon.
\s1 Benediction and Farewell
\p \v 20 Now \add may\add* the God of peace, who through \add the\add* blood of \add the\add* eternal covenant brought back from \add the\add* dead our Lord Jesus, \add that\add* great Shepherd of the sheep, \v 21 equip you with every good \add thing\add*\f + \fr 13:21 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa every good work\f* to do His will. \add And\add* may He accomplish in us\f + \fr 13:21 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa you\f* what \add is\add* pleasing in His sight through Jesus Christ, to whom \add be\add* glory forever \add and\add* ever. Amen.
\p \v 22 I urge you, brothers, to bear with \add my\add* word of exhortation, for I have \add only\add* written to you briefly.
\p \v 23 Be aware \add that\add* our brother Timothy has been released. If he arrives soon, I will come with \add him to see\add* you.
\p \v 24 Greet all your leaders and all the saints.
\p Those from Italy send you greetings.
\p \v 25 Grace \add be\add* with all of you.\f + \fr 13:25 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa Amen.\f*
